    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "human-and-nature",
        "title": "Human and nature",
        "layout": "split",
        "align": "left",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "body": "People are part of nature. We need clean air, water and food, and nature provides them. But climate change is upsetting that balance. What does that mean for animals, for plants — and for us?"
          }
        ],
        "aside": [
          {
            "type": "placeholder",
            "label": "Image to be added",
            "ratio": "652 / 229"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "human-impact",
        "title": "Human impact on nature",
        "layout": "stack",
        "align": "left",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "body": "We have a big impact on nature.\n\nText for this part of the page will be added by the Human Nature team."
          },
          {
            "type": "tiles",
            "items": [
              {
                "title": "Deforestation",
                "text": "Short description to be added.",
                "linkLabel": "",
                "linkUrl": ""
              },
              {
                "title": "Farming and pollution",
                "text": "Short description to be added.",
                "linkLabel": "",
                "linkUrl": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "aside": []
      },
      {
        "id": "natures-impact",
        "title": "Nature's impact on humans",
        "layout": "stack",
        "align": "left",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "body": "Nature gives us food, and it also makes life possible: natural barriers protect our coasts, and plants capture CO₂ and make the oxygen we breathe."
          },
          {
            "type": "circles",
            "items": [
              {
                "label": "Mangroves",
                "image": "",
                "alt": "",
                "text": "Short description to be added."
              },
              {
                "label": "Coral reefs",
                "image": "",
                "alt": "",
                "text": "Short description to be added."
              },
              {
                "label": "Ocean forests: CO₂ capture",
                "image": "",
                "alt": "",
                "text": "Short description to be added."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "aside": []
      },
      {
        "id": "consequences-nature",
        "title": "Consequences for nature",
        "layout": "stack",
        "align": "left",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "body": "When it gets too hot too quickly, animals and plants cannot adjust. What is happening to nature?"
          },
          {
            "type": "circles",
            "items": [
              {
                "label": "Extinction",
                "image": "",
                "alt": "",
                "text": "Animals and plants cannot adjust this fast. Between ice ages and warm periods, species normally had thousands of years to adapt or slowly migrate."
              },
              {
                "label": "Coral dying",
                "image": "",
                "alt": "",
                "text": "Coral dies when sea water gets too warm. At 2 °C of warming, almost all coral reefs (about 99%) could be lost."
              },
              {
                "label": "Ice melting",
                "image": "",
                "alt": "",
                "text": "Melting ice caps raise the sea level, release CO₂ and methane, and remove a bright surface that reflects sunlight. Ice is also home to many species."
              },
              {
                "label": "Ocean acidification",
                "image": "",
                "alt": "",
                "text": "There is less oxygen in the water, and fish species are dying."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "aside": []
      },
      {
        "id": "consequences-humans",
        "title": "Consequences for humans",
        "layout": "stack",
        "align": "left",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "body": "Fewer animals means less food and fewer resources for people. Less coral means less protection for our coasts. More extreme weather means less security for the places where people live."
          },
          {
            "type": "circles",
            "items": [
              {
                "label": "Rising sea level",
